course: chinese-street
lesson: 3
title: Thank you, sorry, excuse me
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At Ming's house good manners are a little different from yours: there is a thank-you, a sorry for when you did wrong, a softer sorry for small slips, and the answers that go with them.
explain: xièxie (thank you) sounds like «SHYEH-shyeh», not «zee-zee» or «shay-shay». The answer is bú kèqi, «don't be so polite», which is a lovely thing to hear.
explain: Bump into someone and you say duìbuqǐ (sorry, I did it); they answer méi guānxi (never mind). Arrive late or forget a name and you say the shy bù hǎoyìsi, with a smile.
explain: Picture a crowded bus: «jiè guò yíxià!» gets you past, qǐng wèn starts a question to a stranger, and máfan nǐ asks a favour, like «sorry to trouble you».
fact: xièxie («SHYEH-shyeh», the first falling, the second light) means thank you; children thank elders for gifts and food, though too many thank-yous between best friends can sound distant.
fact: bú kèqi («BOO KUH-chee») means you're welcome, literally «don't be so polite»; bù turns into rising bú before a falling tone.
fact: bú yòng xiè («BOO yong SHYEH») means no need to thank me, a bit warmer than bú kèqi.
fact: méi shìr («MAY shr», with a Beijing r curl; southern speakers say méi shì) means no problem, it's nothing, or I'm fine after a small fall.
fact: duìbuqǐ («DWAY-boo-chee») is the «I did something wrong» sorry; it is not used for «I'm sorry to hear that».
fact: méi guānxi («MAY gwahn-shee») means never mind, the usual answer to duìbuqǐ; guānxi alone means connections, who you know.
fact: bù hǎoyìsi («boo HOW-ee-sz») is a softer everyday sorry or excuse me for small slips, and also means «oh, I'm embarrassed».
fact: qǐng («ching», dipping) is please as an invitation: qǐng zuò (please sit), qǐng jìn (please come in); kids ask for things with ... hǎo ma? or ... ba instead.
fact: qǐng wèn («ching WUN») means excuse me, may I ask; wèn must fall, because dipping wěn means kiss.
fact: máfan nǐ («MAH-fahn nee») means sorry to trouble you; the answer is bù máfan (no trouble), and hǎo máfan! is a grumpy «what a pain!».
fact: jiè guò yíxià («jyeh GWAW ee-shyah») means excuse me, can I get past; yī is said rising in yíxià because a falling tone follows.
fact: xièxie nǐ («SHYEH-shyeh nee») is a fuller thank you, lovely to a friend's parent who helped you, but funny to your best friend for every crisp.
exercise: How does xièxie sound to an English ear? | SHYEH-shyeh | ZEE-zee, like cheese | SHAY-shay | KEE-kee
exercise: What does bú kèqi literally mean? | Don't be so polite | Don't come in here | No more food, thanks | Not my fault
exercise: Why is it bú kèqi and not bù kèqi? | bù rises to bú before a falling tone | bú is the polite form only used for elders | It is a southern accent | bù means cloth here
exercise: Which answer to xièxie is a bit warmer than bú kèqi? | bú yòng xiè, no need to thank me | duìbuqǐ, sorry | qǐng wèn, may I ask you something | máfan nǐ, sorry to trouble you
exercise: You fall over and Ming asks if you're hurt. What fits? | méi shìr, I'm fine | duìbuqǐ, sorry | bú kèqi, you're welcome | qǐng, please
exercise: When do you say duìbuqǐ? | When you did something wrong, like breaking a toy | When someone tells you that their pet has died | When you want to get past | When you say goodbye
exercise: What is the usual answer to duìbuqǐ? | méi guānxi | bú kèqi | xièxie nǐ | jiè guò yíxià
exercise: guānxi on its own means | Connections, who you know | Never mind, it doesn't matter | Sorry, it was all my own fault | Shut the door behind you, please
exercise: You're five minutes late to Ming's party. What fits best? | bù hǎoyìsi, with a shy smile | duìbuqǐ, duìbuqǐ, duìbuqǐ, again and again | méi guānxi | bú kèqi
exercise: Ming's dad says qǐng zuò. What should you do? | Sit down and say xièxie | Stand up and leave the room | Ask him a question about it | Say duìbuqǐ to him, very loudly
exercise: You need to ask a stranger the way. How do you start? | qǐng wèn | qǐng wěn | máfan bù | wǎn'ān
exercise: What does máfan nǐ mean? | Sorry to trouble you, would you mind | You are a real pain, go away now | Thank you very much | Please sit down
exercise: Ming moans «hǎo máfan!» about homework. What does he mean? | What a pain! | How lovely! | Thank you! | I'm sorry!
exercise: The bus is packed and you need to get off. What do you say? | jiè guò yíxià | méi guānxi | bú yòng xiè | chī le ma
exercise: Why is it yíxià and not yīxià in jiè guò yíxià? | yī rises before a falling tone | It is a different word for «one» | Southern people say it that way | It is a mistake that everyone makes
exercise: Ming's mum fixed your bike. What fits best? | xièxie nǐ, with a big smile | méi guānxi, it doesn't matter | duìbuqǐ | bù xíng
test: «xiexie is like zee-zee right, sounds like cheese» Which is right? | No, it sounds like SHYEH-shyeh | Yes, exactly like zee-zee | Yes, and it means cheese | No, it is said SHAY-SHAY, both falling
test: Ming's granny tells you sad news about her cat. Should you say duìbuqǐ? | No, duìbuqǐ is for something you did wrong | Yes, it is the special sorry for sad news | Yes, it means «poor cat» | No, you should say bú kèqi
test: «oi, ask that lady where the loo is, say qǐng wěn» What happens if you say it with a dip? | You ask «may I kiss» instead of «may I ask» | Nothing, both tones mean ask | You ask her to sell you something | You say good night to her
test: You thank your best mate «xièxie nǐ» for every single crisp. How does it sound? | Funny and a bit distant | Very rude, like an insult | Like you're asking for more | Like you are saying goodbye
test: What would happen if Ming answered your duìbuqǐ with «méi guānxi»? | All good: it means never mind | He is saying he has connections | He is refusing to forgive you yet | He is politely asking you to leave
test: In a game, a polite knight says «qǐng» before every request, like English please. What would a real kid say? | Use ... hǎo ma? or ... ba instead | Shout qǐng even louder every time | Say duìbuqǐ before each and every word | Say nothing at all
test: You tell Ming's dad «máfan nǐ» when asking for help. What will he probably say? | bù máfan, no trouble | hǎo máfan, what a pain | duìbuqǐ, sorry | wǎn'ān, good night
test: «she said méi shì not méi shìr, she's saying it wrong» Who is right? | Both are right; the r curl is Beijing style | Only méi shìr is real Chinese, the other is wrong | Only méi shì is real Chinese | Neither; it is méi xiè
test: «sod off, I'm not saying bù hǎoyìsi for being late, it's just 2 min» What does bù hǎoyìsi do? | It is a small, soft sorry for small slips | It is a big, serious apology for a crime | It is a thank you | It is a goodbye
test: In a cartoon a boy shoves past everyone on the bus shouting «méi guānxi!». What should he have said? | jiè guò yíxià | bú kèqi | xièxie | wǎn'ān, see you
exam: Identify the expression customarily employed to apologise for a wrong one has personally committed. | duìbuqǐ | méi guānxi | bú kèqi | qǐng wèn
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the word qǐng. | It conveys an invitation to the hearer to perform an action, as in qǐng zuò | It is appended to every request in the manner of the English «please» | It is a valediction addressed to elders | It denotes gratitude for a gift
exam: Ascertain the customary reply to an expression of gratitude in Mandarin. | bú kèqi | duìbuqǐ | máfan nǐ | jiè guò yíxià
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression bù hǎoyìsi? | It is a mild apology or expression of embarrassment employed for minor lapses | It is a formal apology reserved for grave offences | It is an expression of condolence upon bereavement | It is a request for passage through a crowd
